"""Labels for 1932 -> 1939 (15th edition, April 1932 -> 16th edition, March 1939).
The 1939 edition letters and sub-divides the notes, folds the 1932 instruction sections (The Batsman, The Bowlers, The Boundary,
Umpires, Code of Signalling; not segmented in the dataset, S38-04) into notes to the relevant Laws, prints the overseas
alterations inline, and tags each Law with '(Introduced/Revised <date>)' (preface, S38-01). Many 'new' notes are therefore moves."""
from _h import make
PAIR = "1932_1939"
L, LABELS = make(PAIR)
ST, UN, NC, SW = "STRUCTURAL", "UNCATEGORISED", "NOT_A_CHANGE", "SEMANTIC_WORDING"
ED = "1932 text = 15th edition (preface April 1932); 1939 text = 16th edition (preface March 1939) (S38-02, S38-01)."
MOVE = ("The 1939 preface says the notes were lettered and sub-divided and that the edition adds notes and interpretations (S38-01); "
        "text found in the 1932 book's unsegmented instruction sections (S38-02, S38-04) is a move, not new.")

L("E001", "New section 'Notes for Scorers and Umpires': (1)(a) scorers must accept the umpires' signals, mutual consultation permitted; (b) two scorers should frequently check the total; (c) umpires wait until a signal is answered and also call distinctly; (d) a recommended method of entering No Balls and Wides in the score sheet (circle/cross in the bowling analysis; runs off the bat from a No Ball count against the bowler); (2) the code of signalling; (3) captains should satisfy themselves of the scores at the close, as errors cannot later be corrected, and accepting the scorers' result means the losing captain has acquiesced in the 'playing out or giving up' of the match (Law 2).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", "The signalling code, the 'wait until a signal has been answered' and 'call distinctly' instructions were in the 1932 book's Code of Signalling (S38-02, not segmented). New are the scorers' duties, the recommended scoring method and the note on agreeing the result. " + MOVE, ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S38-04"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E002", "One-Day Laws notes: 'N.B.—A tie is included...' becomes note (a) with '(See also Law of Cricket No. 2, Note (b).)'; 'the Umpires should decide' -> '(b) ... the umpires shall decide if there is sufficient time left...'; new (c) 'For \"Declaration\" in one-day matches, see Law of Cricket No. 54.'",
  SW, "modal_strength", "medium", "inferred", "should -> shall makes the umpires' fallback decision mandatory in wording; the rest is lettering and cross-references. " + MOVE, ["S38-01"],
  sec=ST, secsub="notes_revision", date=ED)
L("E003", "Note on Law 1 removed: 'A three days' match between two sides of eleven players adjudged first-class shall be regarded as a first-class fixture.'",
  ST, "reorganisation", "high", "derived", "The 1939 book prints the same sentence as a Note under 'Classification of Counties' in its county rules (S38-01), a section the dataset excludes (S38-04). Moved, not dropped.", ["S38-01", "S38-04"], date=ED)
L("E004", "Law 10: tag '(Revised 30th October. 1899.)' added and 'No-ball' -> 'No Ball'; new note (a) 'It is a \"No Ball\" if, when about to deliver the ball, the bowler, seeing the striker out of his ground, throws the ball at the striker's wicket.'",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "The note is a new ruling, not in the 1932 book (S38-02); no source gives a reason. The tag dates the Law's 1899 revision (Rait Kerr serial 113, either umpire may call No ball) and is not a change.", ["S38-01", "S38-02"], date=ED)
L("E005", "Law 11: 'No-Ball' -> 'No Ball', 'bowling crease'/'return crease' capitalised.", NC, "format_artefact", "high", "derived", "Hyphenation and capitalisation only.", ["S18"])
L("E006", "Notes on Law 11 (lettered (a)-(e)): back foot on or outside the return crease: 'the Umpire should call \"No Ball\"' -> 'the umpire shall call \"No Ball,\" unless the other foot is behind the Bowling crease and within the Return crease'; knocking off 'a bail' -> 'a bail or bails'; heel on the ground: 'the Umpire \"No Balls\" him' -> 'the umpire shall \"No Ball\" him'; new (d) 'If the bowler in running up to deliver the ball allows it to slip from his hand before delivery, the umpire, at his discretion, may allow him to bowl again without penalty or interference.'",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "Two real changes: an exception to the back-foot no-ball (fair if the other foot is properly placed) and the new slipped-ball discretion. No source states a reason; Rait Kerr's chronology has no entry before 1947 (serial 133 removes the 'grounded' back-foot requirement, citing umpires' difficulty) (S16).", ["S16", "S38-01"],
  sec=SW, secsub="modal_strength", date=ED)
L("E007", "Notes on Law 12 (Wide): lettered; 'the call should be ignored' -> 'the call shall be ignored'; 'the batsman may be out stumped or run out' -> 'the batsman may be out under Laws 23, 25, 28, 29 and 30'.",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "medium", "inferred", "The list of Laws adds hit wicket, handled the ball and obstructing the field. Hit wicket off a Wide was already stated in the 1932 Notes on Law 16 ('A Batsman may be out \"hit wicket,\" when striking at a \"Wide\"'), and nothing in the Laws exempted a Wide from Laws 29-30, so this collects existing law. Inferred.", ["S38-02", "S38-01"],
  sec=SW, secsub="modal_strength", date=ED)
L("E008", "Law 13 (the Over) and notes: '(For Australia and New Zealand only ... for \"six\" substitute \"eight.\")' printed inline; tag '(Revised 1st May, 1889; 2nd May, 1900; 3rd May, 1922; 7th May, 1924.)'; notes lettered (a)-(g). Moved in from the 1932 Umpires/Bowlers sections: call 'Over' before leaving the wicket; umpires not to call 'Time' before the over is finished; a bowler may change between round and over the wicket (and over/under-arm) in an over 'but he shall give' (1932 'should give') the batsman a fresh guard. New: (a) umpires customarily call 'Over' once both sides treat the ball as dead even if not finally settled; (c) a ball does not count and no runs are scored if both bails are blown off before the stroke or the ball slips from the bowler's hand; 'The umpire is justified in calling \"No Ball\" in the event of contravention of this instruction' (failing to give a fresh guard).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", MOVE + " The eight-ball over for Australia/New Zealand was in the 1932 overseas alterations (S38-02). The customary 'Over' call, the dead-ball cases in (c) and the no-ball sanction for changing the mode of delivery without notice are new and no source gives a reason (searched S16 ch.4-6).", ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S38-04"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED + " Tag dates: 1889 (four to five balls, serial 108), 1900 (six balls, serial 116), 1922/1924 (eight balls in Australia/New Zealand).")
L("E009", "Law 14 (changing ends): tag '(Revised 1st May, 1889.)' added.",
  ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived", "Editorial dating tag; the Law is unchanged. It dates Rait Kerr serial 109 (1 May 1889) (S16, S38-01).", ["S38-01", "S16"], date="1 May 1889 (serial 109).")
L("E010", "Law 16 and notes: cross-reference '(See Laws 13, 23, 28 and 32 and Notes.)' -> '(See Laws 13 and 28 and Notes thereto.)'; notes lettered; the note 'When a run has been made off a \"No Ball,\" the run should be added to the score of the striker although the ball has been caught' moves here from the notes to Law 32; 'A Batsman may be out \"hit wicket,\" when striking at a \"Wide\"' leaves Law 16 (covered by the new Law 12 note listing Laws 23, 25, 28, 29 and 30); OCR 'No Bail'.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "high", "derived", "Notes re-sorted between Laws 12, 16 and 32 with matching cross-references; no rule content added or lost.", ["S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E011", "Note on Law 18: 'In 1911 an agreement was arrived at between the Counties that \"Trial balls, if both batsmen are at the wickets, be not allowed in First Class Matches.\" This arrangement was approved and adopted by the M.C.C.' -> '(a) Trial balls, if both batsmen are at the wickets, are not allowed.'",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "medium", "inferred", "The history of the 1911 County agreement is dropped and the restriction to first-class matches goes. Law 18 itself already said no trial ball 'shall be allowed to any bowler on the ground between the wickets' in any match, so the note now matches the Law. Inferred.", ["S38-01", "S38-02"],
  sec=SW, secsub="qualifier_scope", date=ED)
L("E012", "Notes on Law 2: (a) gains '(See also Law 13, Note (c).)'; new (b) 'A \"tie\" match is one in which the scores are equal when \"time\" has been reached, but only if the match has been played out or if the side to bat last has commenced that innings. In one-day matches a \"tie\" can only apply to the first innings result if the match has not been carried to a further conclusion.'",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "First definition of a tie in the Laws publication; the 1932 book has only 'A tie is included in the words \"played out\"' (One-Day Laws). The condition that the side batting last must have started its innings is new content. No source gives a reason; the 1939 preface states only the general aim of adding notes (S38-01).", ["S38-01"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E013", "Notes on Law 20 (wicket down), lettered (a)-(f): (a) 'The wicket is \"down\" under this Law if any part of either bail is \"struck off\" the top of the wicket' (moved from the Law 21 notes); (d) if one bail has fallen and the ball strikes a stump without displacing the other bail, the wicket is not down unless that stump is struck out of the ground; new (e) if the captains have agreed to dispense with bails in a strong wind, the umpires decide when the wicket is down (a stump need not be struck out of the ground); new (f) umpires may not remake a broken wicket until the ball is dead, though a fieldsman may.",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "Notes (e) and (f) are new rulings, (e) tied to the new Law 6 note allowing play without bails in a high wind; (d) restates the Law. No source states a reason (searched S16 ch.4-6, S38-01 preface).", ["S38-01", "S16"],
  sec=ST, secsub="reorganisation", date=ED)
L("E014", "The ruling that part of a bail is 'struck off' if any part of either bail is disturbed moves from the notes to Law 21 to the notes to Law 20; 'had been' -> 'has been'.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "high", "derived", "Same ruling (M.C.C. Committee, 15 June 1931, S38-03), placed under the Law that defines a wicket 'down'.", ["S38-03", "S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E015", "Notes on Law 21 (bowled): 'An Umpire would be justified in giving the striker out \"Bowled\" if a stump were bowled down, but the bails ... remained' -> '(a) An umpire is justified ... when a stump is bowled down, even though the bails by some mischance remain in position'; the struck-off-bail note leaves for Law 20; new (b) 'The striker having played the ball is out \"Bowled\" if he then kicks the ball on to his wicket.'",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "Note (b) is a new ruling (a kicked ball after the stroke still counts as bowled); no source gives a reason. The rest is a move to Law 20 and a would -> is rewrite.", ["S38-01"],
  sec=ST, secsub="reorganisation", date=ED)
L("E016", "Law 22 cross-reference '(See Notes on Law 32.)' -> '(See Note to Law 32.)'.",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "derived", "Law 32 now has only one note (its no-ball note moved to Law 16).", ["S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E017", "Notes on Law 22 (caught), lettered (a)-(g): 'he would be out' / 'A Batsman would be out if caught off a ball which ... had hit his pad' -> 'A batsman is out...'; new (f) 'The striker is out if the ball is \"hugged to the body of the catcher\" even though he has not touched it with his hands. Should the ball lodge in the fieldsman's clothing, or in the top of the wicket-keeper's pads, this will amount to its being \"hugged to the body of the catcher.\"'; new (g) out 'Caught' although before the catch the ball after touching a fieldsman removed a bail at the non-striker's end with the non-striker out of his ground.",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "Notes (f) and (g) are new rulings on what counts as a catch; (f) echoes the eighteenth-century provision that a catch counts if the ball is 'hugged to the body of the catcher, or be accidentally lodged in his dress' (Rait Kerr, S16), but no source gives a reason for adding it in 1939.", ["S16", "S38-01"],
  sec=SW, secsub="modal_strength", date=ED)
L("E018", "Law 23 (stumped) and notes: OCR 'bail' for 'ball' in the Law; 'pads' -> 'pad'; 'the decision should be \"Stumped\"' -> 'shall be'; 'remove one bail in order to run out or stump a batsman' -> 'in order to stump a batsman' (run out goes to a general Law 28 note); 'It would not be out if he were to knock' -> 'It is not out if he knocks'.",
  SW, "modal_strength", "medium", "inferred", "should -> shall and would -> is make the notes read as rules; the run-out half of the one-bail note moves to Law 28 note (d), which covers any fieldsman. No rule change.", ["S38-01"],
  sec=ST, secsub="reorganisation", date=ED)
L("E019", "Law 24 (l.b.w.): 'if with part of his person he stops the ball, which ... shall have been pitched in a straight line from it to the Striker's wicket and would have hit it' -> 'if with any part of his person (except his hand) which is between wicket and wicket he intercept a ball which ... shall have been pitched in a straight line from the Bowler's wicket to the Striker's wicket or shall have been pitched on the off side of the Striker's wicket and would have hit it'; tag '(Revised 5th May, 1937.)'. Notes: 'three considerations' -> 'four considerations' (pitch on the off side or in line; part of the person other than the hand 'first' intercepted the ball; that part between wicket and wicket at impact); new (d) not out l.b.w. if the ball touched bat or hand first; new (f) 'person' includes dress.",
  "ENJOYMENT", "bowler_batsman_balance", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr serial 128 (5 May 1937): the l.b.w. Law extended to balls pitching on the off side. Ch.4 explains it as the end of a reform campaign against pad-play made possible by improved pitches: Lyttelton's 1928 formula 'was tried out during the following two seasons both in England and Overseas, and having been approved was finally introduced as Law in 1937' (S16). Chandler gives the reason as 'simply to tilt the delicate balance' back toward the bowler and says it was experimental from 1935 (S12). The 1932 book's slip dates the alteration to a Special General Meeting of 5 May 1937 (S38-02).", ["S16", "S12", "S38-02", "S38-01"],
  sec="EXTERNAL_FORCE", secsub="pitch_technology", date="Special General Meeting 5 May 1937 (S38-02 slip; S38-01 tag; Rait Kerr serial 128); experimental from 1935 (S12).")
L("E020", "Notes on Law 25 (hit wicket): would -> is rewrites ('he would be out' -> 'he is out', 'fell ... dislodged' -> 'falls ... dislodges'); new (c) 'If, while playing at the ball, a part of the striker's bat breaks his wicket, he is out.'",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "Note (c) is a new ruling (a broken piece of bat hitting the wicket); no source gives a reason. The rest is tense rewording.", ["S38-01"],
  sec=SW, secsub="clarifying_rewrite", date=ED)
L("E021", "New note (b) to Law 26: 'The correct entry in the score book when the striker is given out under this Law is \"Obstructing the field,\" and the bowler does not get credit for the wicket.'",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "A scoring instruction for an existing mode of dismissal; whether the bowler was earlier credited is not documented in these books. Inferred; no source.", ["S38-01"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E022", "Notes on Law 27 (hit the ball twice): lettered; new (b) 'The spirit of this Law is infringed by the striker, if, after playing the ball, he knocks it back to the bowler, although his wicket is in no danger.'; overthrow runs after a lawful second stroke: 'the batsmen are entitled' -> 'the striker is entitled'; cross-reference 'Note 3' -> 'Note (c)'; the heading 'Either Batsman is Out—' is now segmented before Law 28.",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "Note (b) is new guidance against knocking the ball back to the bowler; 'striker' names who is credited with the runs. No source gives a reason.", ["S38-01"],
  sec=SW, secsub="qualifier_scope", date=ED)
L("E023", "Law 28 (run out): 'No-ball' -> '\"No Ball\"'; tag '1st May, 1912.)' added; cross-reference '(See Notes on Law 20.)' -> '(See Law 31, and Notes to Laws 20 and 35.)'.",
  ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived", "Dating tag (Rait Kerr serial 120, 1 May 1912: cannot be stumped off a No ball but may be run out, S16) and cross-references; Law unchanged.", ["S38-01", "S16"], date="1 May 1912 (serial 120).")
L("E024", "Notes on Law 28: lettered; 'Either batsman can be \"run out\" at any time when the ball is in play by any fieldsman, including the bowler (except as provided above)' -> '(b) At any time when the ball is in play either batsman can be \"run out\" by any fieldsman, including the bowler.'; 'It would not be out if the striker hit' -> 'It is not out if the striker hits'; new (d) 'It is sufficient for a fieldsman, either by throwing, or with the ball in the hand used for the purpose, to remove one bail in order to \"run out\" a batsman.'",
  SW, "qualifier_scope", "low", "inferred", "Dropping '(except as provided above)' removes the reminder of the no-ball exception, which remains in the Law text itself, so the rule is unchanged. Note (d) generalises the 1932 Law 23 note that one bail suffices for the wicket-keeper to run out to any fieldsman (Law 20 notes already said removing the remaining bail suffices).", ["S38-01", "S38-02"],
  sec=ST, secsub="reorganisation", date=ED)
L("E025", "New note to Law 29: 'The correct entry in the score book when a batsman is given out under this Law is \"Handled the ball,\" and the bowler does not get credit for the wicket.'",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "Scoring instruction for an existing mode of dismissal, parallel to the new Law 26 note. Inferred; no source.", ["S38-01"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E026", "Notes on Law 3 (umpires), lettered (a)-(f): 'especially in League and Saturday Matches' -> 'especially in lower grade matches'; moved from the 1932 Umpires section: where to stand ('should ask the permission' -> 'should obtain the permission' of the fielding captain to stand on the off side), agreeing a watch ('should agree' -> 'shall agree'), no right to interfere except on appeal ('or under the provision of Law 43' added), report to the ground manager before play, scorers not to dictate ('should direct' -> 'shall direct'). Real changes: umpires report '30 minutes' before play (1932 'one hour'; 1923 'half an hour'), checking equipment '(using measuring tapes and gauges if necessary)', and may now intervene without appeal for unfair play (Law 43).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", MOVE + " The reporting time and the power to intervene without appeal for unfair play are real changes with no stated cause; reporting times checked in raw OCR of all three books (S38-03 'half an hour', S38-02 'one hour', S38-01 '30 minutes').", ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S38-03", "S38-04"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED, note="The 1923->1932 change half an hour -> one hour was in the unsegmented Umpires section and is not an event of that pair.")
L("E027", "Cross-reference '(See Law 16, Note 5.)' under Law 25 replaced; a new cross-reference '(See Law 26, Note (b).)' appears under Law 30.",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "derived", "Two different cross-references matched by similarity: the hit-wicket-off-a-Wide note they pointed to left Law 16, and Law 30 now points to the new scorebook note on obstructing the field.", ["S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E028", "Notes on Law 32: 'it is not the custom for the remaining batsman to resume his position at the wicket he has left' -> 'the remaining batsman does not resume his position'; the no-ball/caught run note moves to Law 16.",
  SW, "clarifying_rewrite", "medium", "inferred", "Custom restated as plain rule; effect unchanged. The moved note is in the Law 16 notes.", ["S38-01"],
  sec=ST, secsub="reorganisation", date=ED)
L("E029", "Law 33B: tag '(Introduced 3rd May, 1899.)' added.", ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived",
  "Dating tag for Rait Kerr serial 112 (ball dead if it lodges in the batsman's clothing, 3 May 1899) (S16, S38-01).", ["S38-01", "S16"], date="3 May 1899 (serial 112).")
L("E030", "New note to Law 34: 'A lost ball should be replaced by one in a similar condition.'",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "New instruction (cf. the Law 4 note that a ball unfit for play is replaced by one 'as much as possible like the one discarded'). No source gives a reason.", ["S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E031", "Notes on Law 35: (a) gains '(See Law 13, Note (a).)'; 'anyway' -> 'any way'; new (c) 'If the bowler throws the ball at the wicket with the object of running out the batsman at his end, such a throw does not constitute a \"No Ball,\" and the ball not having been delivered does not count as one of the over. If, however, runs result ..., they are to be credited to \"No Balls\" in the score book in accordance with Law 16.'; heading 'GENERAL NOTES ON LAWS 33 A TO 35 INCLUSIVE' and lettering (d)-(h) for the dead-ball notes moved from Laws 33-34.",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "medium", "inferred", "Note (c) explains the Law's own words ('if the Bowler throw at that wicket and any run result, it shall be scored \"No Ball\"'): the throw is not a no-ball delivery but runs go to No Balls. That it does not count in the over is new. Inferred; no source.", ["S38-01"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E032", "Dead-ball list: 'Laws 33a, 33b and Note 3 above, 34, 35, 41 & 50' -> 'Laws 33A, 33B, 34, 35 including Note(f), 41 and 50'.",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "derived", "Re-lettering: the umpire's-clothing note (formerly Note 3 to Laws 33a/b) is now general note (f).", ["S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E033", "Notes on Laws 33a/33b (ball not dead when bails off or after an unsuccessful appeal; on hitting the umpire unless it lodges in his clothing; lodging in a batsman's clothing; ceases to be dead when the bowler begins his run) move to the 'General notes on Laws 33A to 35', with capitalisation changes.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "high", "derived", "Same text, regrouped and lettered (S38-01 preface).", ["S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E034", "New note to Law 36: 'When a batsman has retired owing to illness, injury, or some other unavoidable cause, his innings must be counted as \"Not out.\"'",
  ST, "reorganisation", "high", "derived", "The same sentence is in the 1932 book's 'The Batsman' section (S38-02), which the dataset does not segment (S38-04).", ["S38-02", "S38-04"], date=ED)
L("E035", "Notes on Law 37: '\"it shall be allowed\"' dropped; 'A side which has been short in the field may bat with its full number' and the 1932 Batsman-section note 'A Side which has fielded with ten men, or less, may bat with its full strength provided no disqualification rule applies' merged into '(b) A side may bat with its full number, provided no disqualification rule applies, even though substitutes have been allowed to field.'; 'Many disputes would be avoided if the Captains, before tossing, exchanged lists of the players.' -> 'To prevent any abuse of this provision, and other disputes, the captains, before tossing, should exchange lists of the players.'",
  ST, "consolidation_merge", "medium", "derived", "Two existing notes merged (S38-02); the advice to exchange team lists becomes a 'should' instruction tied to preventing abuse of the full-number provision.", ["S38-02", "S38-01"],
  sec=SW, secsub="modal_strength", date=ED)
L("E036", "Notes on Law 39: lettering '(n)' [OCR for (a)], '(b)'; 'wicket keeper' -> 'wicket-keeper'.", NC, "format_artefact", "high", "derived", "Lettering and hyphenation only.", ["S18"])
L("E037", "Law 4: tag '(Revised 4th May, 1927.)' and cross-reference '(See also Law 3, Note (e).)' added.",
  ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived", "Dating tag for the 1927 smaller ball (Rait Kerr serial 122) and a cross-reference to the umpires' duty to check balls; Law unchanged.", ["S38-01", "S16"], date="4 May 1927 (serial 122).")
L("E038", "Notes on Law 4: (b) 'After 200 runs have been made with a ball in First-class or Minor County matches, the fielding side can demand a new one. This regulation is optional in other grades of cricket. The umpires shall inform the batsmen when a new ball is about to be used.' (1932 Umpires section: 'in the County Competition', 'Umpires should inform'); (c) balls in County matches approved by the captains (moved).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", MOVE + " Real change: the 200-run new ball now applies to Minor County matches and is declared optional elsewhere; 'should' -> 'shall'. Rait Kerr dates the 200-run new ball to 1907 and says it encouraged seam bowling, but gives no reason for this extension (S16).", ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S16"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E039", "Law 41 (fieldsman stopping the ball illegally): 'five runs added to the score; whatever runs may have been made, five only shall be added' -> 'five runs added to the run or runs already made; if no runs have been made five shall be added'; tag '(Revised 6th May, 1936.)'; new note (a) 'The five runs are a penalty and the batsmen do not change ends.'",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "Rait Kerr serial 127 (6 May 1936): 'It is made clear that the penalty of 5 runs for irregular fielding is in addition to any runs already made' (S16); the 1923 book already had a note saying the five runs were added to runs already made (S38-03), dropped in 1932, so the 1936 Law writes that interpretation into the Law. No cause is given. The 1932 slip dates it to a Special General Meeting of 6 May 1936 (S38-02). Note (a), batsmen not changing ends, is new.", ["S16", "S38-03", "S38-02"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date="Special General Meeting 6 May 1936 (S38-02 slip; S38-01 tag; Rait Kerr serial 127).")
L("E040", "Notes on Law 43 (umpires as judges): (a) Fair and Unfair Play: (i) umpires may intervene without appeal for unfair play (new); (ii) appeals by fielders not in a position to judge and tricks such as throwing the ball up (moved from 1932 Umpires; 'should remember' -> 'must remember'); (iii) dissent: umpires first ask the captains to act, then report to the teams' executives; umpires in First-class or Minor County matches report all cases to the M.C.C. Secretary (1932: umpires 'appointed by the Captains of the first and second class Counties' report to the Secretary); (iv) attention to Law 48 (moved); (v) seam-lifting: 'This is not legitimate' -> 'It is illegal'; resin unfair (moved); (vi) NEW: 'The persistent and systematic bowling of fast short-pitched balls at the batsman standing clear of his wicket is \"unfair\"' with a procedure: caution the bowler, then inform the fielding captain and other umpire, then at the first repetition call 'dead ball', ask the captain to take the bowler off for the rest of the innings and report to the batting captain. (b) Ground, Weather and Light: the captains decide fitness, umpires only if the captains disagree or leave it to them; captains inspect after rain; new (v) a batsman at the wickets and the fielding captain consult on weather and light, the umpires deciding if they disagree.",
  "NOVEL_EVENT", "tactic_abuse", "high", "stated", "Note (vi) is the 'direct attack' (bodyline) instruction. Rait Kerr (ch.4, 'Direct attack') says that after the M.C.C. tour of Australia in 1932-3, and after direct-attack methods were used in a few matches in 1934, the M.C.C. obtained the Counties' agreement 'that this type of bowling must cease' and on instructions to umpires defining it as the 'persistent and systematic bowling of fast short-pitched balls at the batsman standing clear of his wicket', which were then made a note to the Law on unfair play; he calls it 'an offence against the spirit of the game' (S16). The rest of the event moves the 1932 Umpires instructions into lettered notes (S38-02, S38-04) with real but unexplained changes to dissent reporting, the power to intervene without appeal and the light procedure.", ["S16", "S38-02", "S38-04", "S38-01"],
  sec=ST, secsub="reorganisation", date="Instructions to umpires agreed with the Counties after the 1934 season (S16 ch.4); first in the Laws publication in the 1939 edition.",
  note="Non-bodyline parts (intervention without appeal, dissent procedure, light consultation by batsman and fielding captain) have no stated cause.")
L("E041", "Notes on Law 44 (boundaries), lettered (a)-(i), moved from the 1932 'The Boundary' section with 'should' -> 'shall' (call a boundary, batsman given out, runs completed shall be scored). New: a fieldsman grounding any part of his body on or over the boundary with the ball in hand makes it a boundary; obstacles struck by the ball are not a boundary 'other than the sight screens'; 'It is not the custom to allow six runs when a ball hits a sight screen full pitch' if it stands on or inside the boundary; if a fieldsman 'involuntarily' causes the ball to reach the boundary only the boundary value counts; a boundary overthrow scores the boundary plus runs completed and the run in progress if the batsmen had crossed when the ball was thrown.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", MOVE + " The grounding, sight-screen, involuntary-boundary and boundary-overthrow rulings are new and no source gives a reason.", ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S38-04"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E042", "Note (c) to Law 45: 'The interval for luncheon shall be 40 minutes. In the event of the last wicket falling within two minutes of the time arranged for luncheon, the game shall be resumed at the usual hour, no allowance being made for the 10 minutes between the innings.' (moved from the 1932 Umpires section, which said 'the seven minutes between the innings').",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "derived", "Moved from the unsegmented 1932 Umpires section (S38-02). Law 45 has always allowed 'ten minutes between each innings'; the 1923 book also said 'ten minutes' (S38-03), so the 1932 'seven' (checked in raw OCR) looks like a mistaken carry-over from the 1931 cut in rolling time, corrected in 1939.", ["S38-02", "S38-03", "S38-01"],
  sec=ST, secsub="drafting_omission", date=ED)
L("E043", "Notes on Law 46 (appeals): 'N.B.' -> (a); (b) out signalled by raising the index finger and umpire calls distinctly (moved from the 1932 signalling code); new 'If the batsman is not out, the umpire shall call \"Not out.\"'; new (c) 'If a batsman thinks he is out and walks away, the umpire is not required to intervene save on appeal or should he consider a decision has been misconstrued'; making sure the batsman understands the decision and (d) altering a decision made in error (moved from Umpires).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", MOVE + " The 'Not out' call and the walking-batsman note are new and no source gives a reason.", ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S38-04"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E044", "Notes on Law 47: 'When the Umpire at the bowler's wicket has given a Batsman \"not out\" the other Umpire may answer any appeal within his jurisdiction' -> 'When either umpire has given a batsman \"Not out\" the other umpire may answer...'; (b) moved from 1932 Umpires: an umpire appeals to the other only when prevented from seeing; 'If he be in any doubt' -> 'If, after consultation, he is still in any doubt', and the principle that 'the actual state of things shall continue' is now attributed to Law 43 (the 1932 text said 'Law 13').",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "Widening the note to either umpire is a real change (the bowler's-end umpire may answer an appeal after the other umpire has said not out). No source gives a reason. 'Law 13' in the 1932 book is an error for Law 43 (the 1923 book has 43, S38-03).", ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S38-03"],
  sec=ST, secsub="reorganisation", date=ED)
L("E045", "Law 48: tag '(Revised 30th October, 1899.)' added.", ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived",
  "Dating tag for Rait Kerr serial 113 (either umpire may call No ball, 30 Oct 1899) (S16, S38-01).", ["S38-01", "S16"], date="30 October 1899 (serial 113).")
L("E046", "Note on 'one short' (batsman taking guard in front of the popping crease) moves from the notes to Law 2 to the notes to Law 49 (short runs).",
  ST, "reorganisation", "high", "derived", "Same text, placed under the Law it concerns.", ["S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E047", "Notes on Law 49: (b) 'When a short run is called the batsmen do not cross over...' (1932 'The Batsman' section: 'it is not customary for the batsmen to cross over'); new (c) 'If two or more runs are attempted, and the umpire calls \"One short,\" only one run is deducted, although the fact that the batsman failed to complete one run shortens the distance necessary to complete the subsequent run.'",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", MOVE + " Note (c) is new and no source gives a reason; 'not customary' -> 'do not' states the custom as a rule.", ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S38-04"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E048", "Law 5: cross-reference '(See also Law 3, Note (e).)' (the 1932 Law had '(See \"Umpires\" page 19.)').",
  ST, "consequential", "high", "derived", "Cross-reference follows the umpires' instructions into the notes to Law 3.", ["S38-01"], date=ED)
L("E049", "New note (b) to Law 50: 'An appeal may not be made after \"Time\" has been called and the bails have been removed by both umpires. \"Time\" shall be called before the players leave the field for any interval of play, as well as at the close of play.'",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "New ruling fixing when appeals end at intervals and close of play; not in the 1932 book (S38-02). No source gives a reason.", ["S38-01", "S38-02"], date=ED)
L("E050", "Law 53: tag '(Revised 10th July, 1894; 2nd May, 1900; 3rd May, 1922: 4th May, 1927.)' added.", ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived",
  "Dating tag; the Law is unchanged. Dates match Rait Kerr serials 111 (1894, printed 'June 10th'), 114 (1900) and 123 (1927) (S16); 1922 is probably the Australian 200-run limit.", ["S38-01", "S16"],
  date="Tag dates 1894/1900/1922/1927 (S38-01).", note="Tag says 10 July 1894; Rait Kerr's chronology prints 10 June 1894 for serial 111.")
L("E051", "Law 54: tag '(Introduced 1st May, 1889. Revised 2nd May, 1900; 2nd May, 1906; 4th May, 1910: 4th May, 1927.)' added; notes lettered; OCR noise ('t6', 'fiftccn', 'commbnucs').",
  ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived", "Dating tag and lettering only; dates match Rait Kerr serials 110, 115, 118, 119 and 124 (S16).", ["S38-01", "S16"], date="Tag dates 1889-1927 (S38-01).")
L("E052", "Law 55: 'two-day'/'one-day' hyphens and quote marks; tag '(Introduced 6th May, 1914. Revised 7th May, 1919.)' added.",
  ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived", "Dating tag (Rait Kerr serial 121, 1914 and 1919) and typography; Law unchanged.", ["S38-01", "S16"], date="6 May 1914 / 7 May 1919 (serial 121).")
L("E053", "Law 6 (wickets): tag '(Revised 6th May, 1931.)', cross-reference '(See also Law 3, Note (e).)'; note (a) capitalisation; new (b) 'The use of stumps with metal heads or fittings is a source of danger, and though permissible should not be encouraged.'; new (c) 'In a high wind, the captains may agree, with the approval of the umpires, to dispense with the use of bails, in which case Law 20 must be modified.'",
  "EXTERNAL_FORCE", "medical_safety", "medium", "stated", "The note itself gives its reason: metal-headed or metal-fitted stumps are 'a source of danger' (S38-01). Note (c), playing without bails in a high wind, is a separate new provision with no stated cause. The tag dates the 1931 enlargement (serial 125).", ["S38-01"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED)
L("E054", "Law 7: tag '(Revised 7th May, 1902.)' added.", ST, "notes_revision", "high", "derived",
  "Dating tag for Rait Kerr serial 117 (bowling crease 8 ft 8 in, 7 May 1902) (S16, S38-01).", ["S38-01", "S16"], date="7 May 1902 (serial 117).")
L("E055", "Note on Law 8: '4 feet' -> 'four feet', 'popping' capitalised.", NC, "format_artefact", "high", "derived", "Numeral written as a word; same value.", ["S18"])
L("E056", "Law 9 (the ground) and notes: tag '(Revised 6th May, 1931.)'; notes lettered; new (a)(i) 'Prior to tossing for innings, the executive of the ground on which a match is to be played is responsible for the selection and preparation of the wicket.'; (a)(ii) the batting side's right to seven minutes' rolling now says 'before the commencement of each day's play' (1932 note: 'each innings and of each day's play'; the Law still says each innings); umpires' supervision of rolling merged with the 1932 Umpires instruction (not obliged to stand near the wicket; rolling completed in time); covering note loses 'The M.C.C. have advised that'; foot-holes cleaned and filled with sawdust (moved from Umpires); 'ADDITIONAL LAW FOR AUSTRALIA, NEW ZEALAND AND SOUTH AFRICA. 9*' (rolling up to ten minutes after rain) printed inline, '(Introduced 3rd May, 1922. Application extended to New Zealand 7th May, 1924, and to South Africa 2nd May, 1928.)', with a new note that a responsible officer supervises such rolling in first-class matches.",
  ST, "reorganisation", "medium", "inferred", MOVE + " Law 9* was in the 1932 overseas alterations (S38-02). New: the ground executive's responsibility for preparing the pitch, the supervising officer for Law 9* rolling, and the covering regulation stated as a note rather than M.C.C. advice; no source gives reasons. Rait Kerr notes that in 1938 the Counties accepted M.C.C. instructions to groundsmen on pitch preparation (ch.4, S16), which may be related but he does not tie it to this note.", ["S38-01", "S38-02", "S38-04", "S16"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date=ED + " Law 9* dated 1922/1924/1928 by its tag.")
